    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-theresa.jpg" alt="Maria Theresa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiserin_Maria_Theresia_(HRR).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin van Meytens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sultanate of Zanzibar was the African half of the maritime empire built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-bu-said-dynasty">Al Bu Said dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oman">Oman</a>. Omani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabs">Arabs</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fort-jesus">Fort Jesus</a> at Mombasa from the Portuguese in 1698 and asserted a loose overlordship over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#swahili">Swahili</a> towns of the East African coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#said-bin-sultan">Sayyid Said bin Sultan</a>, ruler of Oman from 1806, turned that claim into an empire: he subdued the rival Mazrui governors of Mombasa by 1837, encouraged the planting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cloves">cloves</a> on Zanzibar and Pemba, and from about 1840 made Zanzibar town his principal residence. Under him the island became the hub of a commercial system reaching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#somalis">Somali</a> coast to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mozambique">Mozambique</a> and inland to the great lakes. When Said died at sea in 1856, his sons divided his realm: Thuwaini took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muscat">Muscat</a> and Oman, while Majid bin Said became sultan of Zanzibar and the African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dominion">dominions</a>. Thuwaini prepared an expedition to assert his claim, and Barghash, another brother, attempted a coup in Zanzibar in 1859, but British intervention kept Majid on the throne. In 1861 the Canning Award, issued by Lord Canning as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>, confirmed the separation of the two states, with Zanzibar to pay Oman an annual subsidy of 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> thalers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-gulf.jpg" alt="Persian Gulf" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zanzibar’s wealth rested on plantations and long-distance trade. Clove trees, introduced early in the nineteenth century, spread across Zanzibar and especially Pemba, and by the middle of the century the islands produced most of the world’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cloves">cloves</a>. The plantations, owned largely by Omani Arab families, were worked by enslaved Africans brought from the mainland, as were the coconut and grain estates of the coast. Caravans organised by Arab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#swahili">Swahili</a> and Nyamwezi traders and financed by Indian merchants travelled inland to Tabora, Ujiji on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> and beyond, returning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> and captives; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a>, the most famous of these traders, built a personal domain in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#congo-basin">Congo basin</a>. Many thousands of enslaved people a year passed through the Zanzibar slave market, some kept on the islands and the coast, others shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Indian merchants and financiers, both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim, controlled credit and, through the customs farm, much of the sultan’s revenue. Zanzibar town grew into a cosmopolitan port of coral-stone houses, mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bazaar">bazaars</a> and consulates, where Arab, Swahili, Indian, Comorian and African communities mixed and Swahili spread as the language of trade across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a>. European and American ships called for ivory, cloves and gum copal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-ocean.jpg" alt="Indian Ocean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Ocean-CIA_WFB_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">United States Central Intelligence Agency, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate was never a strong military power, and its authority on the mainland rested more on commerce and prestige than on force. Its garrisons in the coastal towns were small and largely made up of Baluchi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> recruited from the Makran coast, along with slave soldiers and armed retainers of Omani clans. The sultans owned a handful of armed ships and steamers, used mainly to carry troops and officials along the coast, but naval mastery in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>, whose cruisers patrolled the coast against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In 1877 Barghash commissioned Lloyd Mathews, a lieutenant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, to raise a regular force trained on European lines, which grew to more than a thousand men and later formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">protectorate</a>’s armed forces. These troops helped assert the sultan’s control over parts of the coast in the late 1870s and 1880s, but they were no match for European navies. In August 1885 a German squadron anchored off Zanzibar and forced Barghash to accept German claims in the interior. The weakness was exposed most starkly on 27 August 1896, when British warships bombarded the palace of the usurping sultan Khalid bin Barghash and ended the Anglo-Zanzibar War in less than an hour.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultan was in principle an absolute ruler, governing through members of the Al Bu Said family, Omani notables, Islamic judges (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qadi">qadis</a></em>) and governors (<em>liwalis</em>) appointed to coastal towns from the Benadir coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#somalia">Somalia</a> to Kilwa. In practice authority on the mainland was patchy: some towns accepted Zanzibari garrisons and customs posts, while inland the sultan’s name counted only where traders carried it. Revenue came above all from customs duties on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cloves">cloves</a>, slaves and imports, which were farmed out to Indian firms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> Majid (1856–1870) began building Dar es Salaam on the mainland in the 1860s. Barghash bin Said (1870–1888) was the most ambitious ruler: he built palaces including the Beit al-Ajaib, the House of Wonders, completed in 1883, brought piped water and steamships to Zanzibar, and supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#printing-press">printing</a> of Islamic texts. At the same time British influence grew steadily, exercised through consuls such as Sir John Kirk, whose advice carried great weight with the sultan. Under pressure from the mission of Sir Bartle Frere and the threat of a British naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naval-blockade">blockade</a>, Barghash signed the treaty of 1873 that closed the Zanzibar slave market and banned the shipping of slaves from the mainland. After 1890 real power passed to British officials, among them Lloyd Mathews, who served as the sultan’s first minister.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#david-livingstone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-livingstone.jpg" alt="David Livingstone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Livingstone_by_Thomas_Annan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Annan / Adam Cuerden, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zanzibar was the commercial capital of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a>, and its influence reached far beyond the islands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> it financed carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#swahili">Swahili</a> language, firearms, cloth and new crops deep into the interior, linking the peoples of present-day Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, Malawi and the eastern Congo to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> economy. The same routes devastated many inland societies through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> and violence, and the trade became a central theme of the British anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">slavery</a> movement. Zanzibar was also the starting point for European exploration of the interior: Richard Burton, John Hanning Speke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> all outfitted their expeditions in the town, often with the help of its merchants. The British campaign against the Zanzibar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, culminating in the closure of the market in 1873 and the abolition of the legal status of slavery in 1897, shaped British policy across East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>, and Christ Church Cathedral now stands on the site of the old market. Stone Town, with its carved doors and coral-stone architecture, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The memoirs of Sayyida Salme, a daughter of Said who eloped with a German merchant and wrote as Emily Ruete, give a rare inside view of the sultan’s household.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#jamshid-bin-abdullah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamshid-bin-abdullah.jpg" alt="Jamshid bin Abdullah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#jamshid-bin-abdullah">Jamshid bin Abdullah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamshid_bin_Abdullah_of_Zanzibar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate’s mainland empire was dismantled in the European partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>. Carl Peters made treaties with chiefs in the interior in 1884, and the German naval demonstration of 1885 forced Barghash to recognise them. An Anglo-German agreement of 1886 limited the sultan’s mainland domain to a coastal strip ten miles deep, which was soon leased out: the German share provoked the Abushiri revolt of 1888–1889 and was bought outright by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> in 1890, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> administered the northern strip and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> took over the Benadir ports. By the Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty of 1 July 1890 Germany recognised British claims, and Zanzibar and Pemba became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">protectorate</a> later that year. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> Hamad bin Thuwaini died in August 1896 and Khalid bin Barghash seized the palace without British approval, a brief bombardment installed a compliant successor. The sultans reigned for almost seven more decades under British residents, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khalifa">Khalifa</a> bin Harub (1911–1960), while the economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cloves">cloves</a> and the Arab elite kept its privileges. After bitterly contested elections between 1957 and 1963, Zanzibar became independent on 10 December 1963 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jamshid-bin-abdullah">Sultan Jamshid bin Abdullah</a>. On 12 January 1964 a revolution overthrew him, killed thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabs">Arabs</a> and South Asians and created the People’s Republic of Zanzibar, which joined Tanganyika in April 1964 to form Tanzania.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sultanate of Zanzibar was the commercial capital of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a>, an Omani Arab state whose clove plantations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> caravans and slave market tied the interior to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> world, and whose fall under British protection and the 1964 revolution shaped modern Tanzania.</em></p>

